Let me verify: the loop is:
```c
for (i = 0; i < naddrs; i++)
{
    hostent->h_addr_list[i] = (char*)&addrs[i];
    memcpy(hostent->h_addr_list[i], &(((struct sockaddr_in *)next->ai_addr)->sin_addr), sizeof(struct in_addr));
    if (naddrttls)
    {
        if(next->ai_ttl > cname_ttl)
          addrttls[i].ttl = cname_ttl;
        else
          addrttls[i].ttl = next->ai_ttl;

        memcpy(&addrttls[i].ipaddr, &(((struct sockaddr_in *)next->ai_addr)->sin_addr), sizeof(struct in_addr));
    }
    next = next->ai_next;
}
```

And `naddrs` is computed by counting all A record nodes:
```c
next = ai.nodes;
while (next)
{
    ++naddrs;
    next = next->ai_next;
}
```

The caller passes `count = 5` via `naddrttls`, meaning "I have room for 5 entries." But the function never checks if `naddrs > *naddrttls` before writing! It just writes all `naddrs` entries to `addrttls[i]` without checking the limit.

Similarly for `ares_parse_aaaa_reply`:
```c
struct ares_addr6ttl info6[5];
count = 5;
ares_parse_aaaa_reply(data, size, &host, info6, &count);
```

Same pattern - if there are more than 5 AAAA records, it overflows.

This is the classic OSS-Fuzz c-ares bug! The fix would be to limit `naddrs` to `*naddrttls` when writing to the `addrttls` array.

So to trigger this, I need to craft a DNS response with more than 5 A records (or AAAA records). The fuzzer passes an array of 5 `ares_addrttl` structs. If the DNS response contains 6+ A records, it will write past the end of the stack array.

Let me craft a DNS response packet with:
- Header: QR=1, QDCOUNT=1, ANCOUNT=6 (or more)
- Question section: a domain name, type A, class IN
- Answer section: 6+ A records pointing to the same domain

Let me construct this packet.

